description:
flout labs is seeking a Product Operations / Business Analyst to support a fast\-moving insurance claims software project.




 This is not a traditional corporate BA role.




 I need someone who can work with users, handle ambiguity, stay calm under pressure, and relentlessly search for the truth of what is actually needed.




 The work includes:




 \- speaking with users and stakeholders;


 \- capturing what they actually mean, not just what they first say;


 \- turning messy conversations into clear requirements;


 \- researching workflows, vendors, APIs, data sources, and edge cases;


 \- preparing UAT scripts and capturing test evidence;


 \- separating bugs, hardening work, existing scope, and new scope;


 \- drafting user stories, acceptance criteria, and decision\-ready notes;


 \- tracking open questions, blockers, owners, and follow\-ups.




 The right person will be an amazing people person: warm, curious, clear, and persistent without being pushy.




 You should be able to ask good questions, listen carefully, spot contradictions, and keep digging until the real job\-to\-be\-done becomes clear.




 You should also be comfortable using modern tools to research, write, organise, and accelerate your own work. We care less about formal process and more about whether you can produce useful, structured outputs quickly.




 Insurance experience is useful but not required. Software/product experience is useful. Strong written English is required.




 Good fit if you are:




 \- calm under pressure;


 \- highly organised;


 \- good with people;


 \- comfortable with ambiguity;


 \- honest about what you know and don’t know;


 \- relentless about finding the real problem;


 \- able to produce clear notes, requirements, UAT scripts, and research summaries.




 This will start with a small paid test task and may become ongoing part\-time work.




 Remote/offshore is fine.




 Please send a short note with your experience, hourly rate, availability, and one example of structured work you’ve produced: a requirement note, UAT script, research summary, or similar.
